===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (something) to be [[physically]] [[disorder#Verb|disordered]] or [[disturb#Verb|disturbed]]; to cause [[confusion]].
##: {{syn|en|derange|disturb|unsettle|Thesaurus:confuse}}
##* {{RQ:John Heywood Pardoner|sig=B.i.|page=8|passage=Mary therefore the more knaue art thou I ſay / That '''perturbeſt''' the worde of god I ſay {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Grafton Chronicle|volume=II|chapter=[[w:Henry II of England|Henry the Seconde]]|page=69|passage=The Nobles ſtandyng by hearyng him thus ſpeake were greatly agreeued with him, notyng in him arrogancy and wilfulneſſe, in '''perturbyng''' and refuſyng ſuch an honeſt order of agreement: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|chapter=Concerning the Vulgar Opinion that the Earth was Slenderly Peopled before the Floud|page=295|passage=[T]he grounds and firſt inventions of Poeticall fables, {{...}} vvere alſo taken up by hiſtoricall VVriters, '''perturbing''' the Chaldean and Ægyptian Records vvith fabulous additions, and confounding their names and ſtories, vvith their ovvne inventions.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1730|author=[[w:Thomas Fuller (writer)|Thomas Fuller]]|chapter=Of Continual Fevers, Caused by Matter Taken into the Blood ab extra|title=Exanthematologia: Or, An Attempt to Give a Rational Account of Eruptive Fevers, Especially of the Measles and Small Pox.{{nb...|In Two Parts. Part I. Of the Blood, the Air, Venoms, Infection; Fevers of All Kinds in General, and of Such in Particular as are Morbillous with Spots, or Variolous with Pustles; and in Specie, of Most of the Spurious Sorts Mentioned in Books, and Met with in Practice; with Their Varieties, Descriptions, Names, &c. but More Professedly and Fully of the True Measles. To which is Prefix’d, a Delineation and Comparison of the Old and New Philosophy. In a Dialogue. By an Unknown Hand. Part II. Of the Small-pox, its Nature, Genius, and Production; of Its Causes, Differences, Diagnostic and Prognostic Signs; also of Prevention, Management, Medicines, and Methods to be Observed and Used in the Cure of the Same; and its Symptoms, from its First Invasion, through All its Periods and Progress, to its Conclusion: And Lastly, of the Bleeding Pox. To which is Added, an Appendix Concerning Inoculation. The Whole Illustrated with Many New, Curious, and Useful Particulars.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} {{w|Charles Rivington}}, and Stephen Austen,{{nb...|in St. Paul’s Church-yard.}}|pages=114–115|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b3040888x/page/114/mode/1up|oclc=1509642809|passage=If it {{quote-gloss|benign matter}} be as yet ſeated in the Lymph, and ſcarce got in among the Globules, it vvill notvvithſtanding provoke, and '''perturb''' the vital Spirits, and ſo exagitate them as to ſtir up an Efferveſcence, vvhich ceaſeth again as ſoon as the Matter can be concocted and throvvn out by inſenſible Tranſpiration, Svveat, Urine, Stools, &c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|author=Abū Muẖammad bin Yusuf bin Mu,ayyid-i-Niz̤āmu-’d-Dīn [''i.e.'', {{w|Nizami Ganjavi}}]|chapter=Canto XXVIII. Sikandar’s Written Reply to Dara.|translator=[[w:Henry Wilberforce Clarke|H[enry] Wilberforce Clarke]]|title=[[w:Iskandarnameh (Nizami)|The Sikandar Nāma,e Barȧ, or Book of Alexander the Great,{{nb...|Written A.D. 1200, by Abū Muẖammad bin Yusuf bin Mu,ayyid-i-Niz̤āmu-’d-Dīn, Translated for the First Time out of the Persian into Prose, with Critical and Explanatory Remarks, with an Introductory Preface, and with a Life of the Author, Collected from Various Persian Sources}}]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:W. H. Allen & Co.|W[illiam] H[oughton] Allen & Co.]],{{nb...|13 Waterloo Place, S.W. publishers to the India Office.}}|line=67|page=313|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sikandarnmaebara00nimg/page/n350/mode/1up|oclc=1342242869|passage=[T]hou '''perturbest''' my place; / Takest from me the country of my forefathers; {{...}}}}
## To disturb (someone, their [[mind#Noun|mind]], etc.) [[mentally]]; to [[bother#Verb|bother]], [[trouble#Verb|trouble]], [[upset#Verb|upset]].
##: {{syn|en|agitate|distress|unsettle|Thesaurus:upset}}
##: {{ant|en|Thesaurus:comfort}}
##* {{RQ:Jonson Magnetic Lady|act=I|scene=i|page=10|passage={{...}} I have often found / The truth thereof, in my private paſſions: / For I doe never feele my ſelfe '''perturb'd''' / VVith any generall vvords 'gainſt my profeſſion, / They doe avvake, and ſtirre me: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Woodstock|volume=I|chapter=V|page=146|passage=He remembered how, {{...}} his childish imagination was '''perturbed''' at a phenomenon, for which he could not account.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author=w:John Chrysostom|chapter=Homily XXIII. [[w:Second Epistle to the Corinthians|2 Cor{{quote-gloss|inthians}}]] xi. 1.|editor=J. F. Christie|translator=J. Ashworth|title=The Homilies of S. John Chrysostom, Archbishop of Constantinople, on the Second Epistle of St. Paul the Apostle to the Corinthians.{{nb...|Translated, with Notes and Indices.}}|series=[[w:Library of the Fathers|A Library of Fathers of the Holy Catholic Church, anterior to the Divison of the East and West]]|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=[[w:John Henry Parker (writer)|John Henry Parker]]; London: [[w:Rivington (publishers)|F[rancis] and J[ohn] Rivington]]|page=269|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/homiliesofsjohnc27john/page/269/mode/1up|oclc=856329341|passage=[I]t is very easy to abstain from this sin {{quote-gloss|the desire of money}}. For here it is not any natural desire that '''perturbeth''' the mind, but it ariseth from wilful negligence.}}
##* {{RQ:Doyle Case-Book of Sherlock Holmes|year=1927|page=198|passage="Did he seem to you much '''perturbed'''?" / "Mr. Gibson is a very strong, self-contained man. I do not think that he would ever show his emotions on the surface. But I, who knew him so well, could see that he was deeply concerned."}}
## {{lb|en|astronomy}} Of a [[celestial body]]: to [[modify]] the [[motion#Noun|motion]] or [[orbit#Noun|orbit]] of (another celestial body) by [[exert]]ing a [[gravitational force]]; hence {{lb|en|physics}}, to [[slightly]] modify (the motion of an [[object#Noun|object]]).
## {{lb|en|mathematics}} To slightly modify (a [[set#Noun|set]] of [[equation]]s or their [[solution#Noun|solutions]]), [[produce#Verb|producing]] [[deviation]]s from a [[simple#Adjective|simple]], [[easily]] [[solvable]] [[problem#Noun|problem]], in order to [[find#Verb|find]] an [[approximate#Adjective|approximate]] solution to a problem that is more [[difficult#Adjective|difficult]] to [[solve#Verb|solve]] or otherwise [[unsolvable#Adjective|unsolvable]].
## {{lb|en|sciences}} To [[influence#Verb|influence]] (a [[process#Noun|process]] or [[system]]) so that it [[deviate#Verb|deviates]] from its [[normal#Adjective|normal]] [[state#Noun|state]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To bother, to disturb, to trouble.
#: {{syn|en|agitate}}
#* {{RQ:Virgil Phaer Eneidos|sig=&#91;R.iij.&#93;|page=140|passage=Thy ghoſt O father ſweete, thy greuous ghoſt, / '''Perturbing''' in my dremes hath me compeld to ſee this coaſt.}}
#* {{RQ:Lowell Cathedral|pages=11–12|pageref=12|passage=This growth original of virgin soil, / By fascination felt in opposites, / Pleases and shocks, entices and '''perturbs'''.}}

